{
  "name": "How to Make Sure AI Can 'Read' My Website Content",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1: Identify What Information Needs Schema Markup",
      "text": "First, pinpoint the key entities and information on each page that AI systems would find valuable. This includes services, products, locations, FAQs, articles, and reviews. For example, on a service page, you'd want to mark up the service name, description, and pricing. On an article page, you'd mark up the article title, author, and publication date. The goal is to provide explicit context for every piece of important information, telling AI exactly what it's looking at. Without this clear identification, your content is just generic text, not a machine-readable data point. Ensure the marked-up information is always visible and accurate on the page itself.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 2: Implement JSON-LD Schema Markup",
      "text": "Google strongly recommends JSON-LD for structured data, so focus your efforts there. This involves embedding a specific script on your page that defines elements like `Service`, `FAQPage`, `Article`, or `LocalBusiness`. Each type has required properties you absolutely can't skip, like the `name` and `description` for a `Service`. For example, a `LocalBusiness` schema needs `name`, `address`, and `telephone` to be valid. You're essentially creating a rich data layer that gives AI a direct roadmap to your content. Avoid marking up hidden content or information unrelated to the page, as this is a common mistake.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 2
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 3: Validate Your Structured Data",
      "text": "Once you've implemented the schema, immediately validate it. Google's Rich Results Test is your go-to for checking if your markup is eligible for specific search features. For a broader `schema.org` check, the Schema Markup Validator is incredibly useful. These tools will flag any errors, missing required properties, or syntax issues. It's like a spell-check for your AI language; don't skip it. Validation confirms your implementation is technically sound, which is a big step toward AI readability.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 3
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 4: Monitor and Refine with Search Console",
      "text": "After validation and deployment (start with a limited set of pages if possible), keep a close eye on your performance in Google Search Console. Inspect the URLs you've marked up and look for any new structured data enhancements or warnings. Google won't recrawl and process changes instantly, so be patient, but regular monitoring helps you identify if your schema is being picked up correctly and if any issues arise. Comparing performance over several months can give you insights into how your changes are impacting visibility. This ongoing vigilance ensures your AI-friendly efforts stay effective and adapt as AI systems evolve.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 4
    }
  ],
  "@type": "HowTo",
